index c3f4f1b..2df9b54 100644
--- a/benchmark/README.md
+++ b/benchmark/README.md
@@ -49,3 +49,31 @@ Reference points on the same suites: `always-approve` catches 0% with 0% safe es
 
 Add `benchmark/cases/<name>.json` with `{ "name", "author", "cases": [{ id, category, set, type, change }] }`
 (`type` is `risky` or `safe`), or submit a sealed commitment manifest `<name>.hashes.json` with pre-committed SHA-256 hashes.
+
+## Sealed suites (hash commitments)
+
+A suite is only a fair test if the evaluator's author has never seen it. `benchmark/commit.ts`
+creates one:
+
+```bash
+npx tsx benchmark/commit.ts my_cases.json      # writes <name>.hashes.json and <name>.revealed.json
+```
+
+- Commit **only** `<name>.hashes.json`. Each case is hashed as `SHA-256("<salt>:<id>:<type>:<change>")`
+  with a random per-case salt, and the hashes are combined into a Merkle root (scheme documented in
+  the manifest). The git commit time is the real timestamp.
+- `<name>.revealed.json` holds the plaintext and salts. It is git-ignored. Keep it away from any
+  person or model that edits the evaluator until you are ready to score.
+- Score with `npm run benchmark -- --reveal <name>.revealed.json`. The runner checks every hash and
+  the Merkle root first, and refuses to score a reveal that has no matching commitment.
+- A set is spent once its results have guided a rule change. Make a new one with `commit.ts`.
+
+`community_seed_v1` predates this scheme: its Merkle algorithm is undocumented and nobody has
+verified its plaintext, so treat it as unverified. `community_seed_v2` follows the scheme above.
+
+## Reading fail-closed results
+
+`baseline:always-escalate` flags every change for human review. If an evaluator scores like it
+(100% risky caught, 100% of safe changes escalated), it is not discriminating; it is just
+escalating everything. Compare against it, especially on sealed suites.
+
